{"name":"Workshop Blue","hex":"#02667b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"workshop","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A room, especially one which is not particularly large, used for manufacturing or other light industrial work.","A brief, intensive course of education for a small group, emphasizing interaction and practical problem solving."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To help a playwright revise a draft of (a play) by rehearsing it with actors and critiquing the results.","To work on or revise something, especially collaboratively, in a workshop."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/workshop"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Workshop","blocks":[{"text":"Beginning with the Industrial Revolution era, a workshop may be a room, rooms or building which provides both the area and tools that may be required for the manufacture or repair of manufactured goods. Workshops were the only places of production until the advent of industrialization and the development of larger factories. In the 20th and 21st century, many Western homes contained a workshop in either the garage, basement, or an external shed. Home workshops typically contain a workbench, hand tools, power tools, and other hardware. Along with the practical application of repairing goods, workshops are often used to tinker and make prototypes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/EWM_shop_2007.jpg/330px-EWM_shop_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workshop"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Metal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg/500px-Metal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Metal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg/1280px-Metal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Metal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg/500px-Metal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Metal workers at workshop in Tampere, Finland in 1955","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMetal_workers_in_Tampere_workshop_1955.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Railway_workshop_museum_exhibition_in_Ljubljana%2C_Slovenia.jpg/500px-Railway_workshop_museum_exhibition_in_Ljubljana%2C_Slovenia.jpg?utm_sour